referenceSolutions.java:
class Solution {
  public int[] catalogBranchesProofStrip(TreeNode root, int k) {
    TreeNode a=root; List<Integer> out=new ArrayList<>();
    {class Walk {void visit(TreeNode node){if(node==null)return;if(node.val>k)visit(node.left);if(node.val>=k&&node.val<=k+12)out.add(node.val);if(node.val<k+12)visit(node.right);}}new Walk().visit(a);}
    return out.stream().mapToInt(Integer::intValue).toArray();
  }
}

privateData.editorialMd:
The working idea

The input is a binary search tree with distinct values. Produce its values in the inclusive range [k,k+12], in ascending order, pruning subtrees that cannot meet that range.

Return the entire derived sequence, preserving its order.

Why this works

Each derived value follows the unit's stated order and includes exactly the items allowed by its definition. The receipt then folds or filters those values without changing their meaning. Check the empty, singleton, repeated and maximum-size inputs before trusting the final total.

Target time O(h + r); space O(n).

